<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Agram-schmidt-process> <http://example.org/ch15/alias> "Gram-Schmidt orthogonalization process" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Agram-schmidt-process> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Agram-schmidt-process/In%20the%20Gram-Schmidt%20process%20%2815.15%29%2C%20we%20construct%20the%20element%20%24y_%7Br%2B1%7D%24%20by%20subtracting%20from%20%24x_%7Br%2B1%7D%24%20the%20projection%20of%20%24x_%7Br%2B1%7D%24%20along%20each%20of%20the%20earlier%20elements%20%24y_%7B1%7D%2C%5Cldots%2Cy_%7Br%7D%24%20.> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Agram-schmidt-process> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Agram-schmidt-process/The%20construction%20is%20called%20the%20Gram-Schmidt%20orthogonalization%20process%2C%20in%20honor%20of%20J.%20P.%20Gram> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Agram-schmidt-process> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Agram-schmidt-process/These%20formulas%20describe%20the%20Gram-Schmidt%20process%20for%20constructing%20an%20orthogonal%20set%20of%20nonzero%20elements%20%24y_%7B1%7D%2C%20%5Cldots%2C%20y_%7Bk%7D%24%20which%20spans%20the%20same%20subspace%20as%20a%20given%20independent%20set> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Agram-schmidt-process> <http://example.org/ch15/name_en> "Gram-Schmidt process"@en .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Agram-schmidt-process> <http://example.org/ch15/name_zh> "Gram-Schmidt 正交化过程"@zh .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Agram-schmidt-process> <http://example.org/ch15/node_type> "method" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Agram-schmidt-process> <http://example.org/ch15/section> "15.13" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Agram-schmidt-process> <http://example.org/ch15/statement> "The explicit construction y_1 = x_1 and y_{r+1} = x_{r+1} - sum_{i=1}^{r} ((x_{r+1}, y_i)/(y_i, y_i)) y_i (formula 15.15), which turns an independent set x_1, ..., x_k in a Euclidean space into an orthogonal set of nonzero elements y_1, ..., y_k spanning the same subspace. Each step subtracts from x_{r+1} its projection along every earlier y_i." .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Alegendre-polynomials> <http://example.org/ch15/alias> "P_n" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Alegendre-polynomials> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Alegendre-polynomials/EXAMPLE%202.%20The%20Legendre%20polynomials.%20In%20the%20linear%20space%20of%20all%20polynomials%2C%20with%20the%20inner%20product%20%24%28x%2C%20y%29%20%3D%20%5Cint_%7B-1%7D%5E%7B1%7D%20x%28t%29%20y%28t%29%20dt%24> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Alegendre-polynomials> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Alegendre-polynomials/The%20first%20few%20polynomials%20are%20easily%20calculated%20by%20the%20Gram-Schmidt%20process.> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Alegendre-polynomials> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Alegendre-polynomials/When%20the%20orthogonalization%20theorem%20is%20applied%20to%20this%20sequence%20it%20yields%20another%20sequence%20of%20polynomials%20%24y_0%2C%20y_1%2C%20y_2%2C%20%5Cldots%24> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Alegendre-polynomials> <http://example.org/ch15/name_en> "Legendre polynomials"@en .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Alegendre-polynomials> <http://example.org/ch15/name_zh> "Legendre 多项式"@zh .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Alegendre-polynomials> <http://example.org/ch15/node_type> "concept" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Alegendre-polynomials> <http://example.org/ch15/section> "15.13" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Alegendre-polynomials> <http://example.org/ch15/statement> "Applying the orthogonalization theorem to the sequence x_n(t) = t^n in the space of all polynomials with inner product (x, y) = int_{-1}^{1} x(t) y(t) dt yields orthogonal polynomials y_0, y_1, y_2, ...; the Legendre polynomials are their rescalings P_n(t) = ((2n)!/(2^n (n!)^2)) y_n(t) = (1/(2^n n!)) d^n/dt^n (t^2 - 1)^n. The first few are y_0 = 1, y_1 = t, y_2 = t^2 - 1/3." .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Anormalized-legendre-polynomials> <http://example.org/ch15/alias> "phi_n" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Anormalized-legendre-polynomials> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Anormalized-legendre-polynomials/The%20polynomials%20in%20the%20corresponding%20orthonormal%20sequence%20%24%5Cvarphi_%7B0%7D%24%20%2C%20%24%5Cvarphi_%7B1%7D%24%20%2C%20%24%5Cvarphi_%7B2%7D%24%20%2C%20%24%5Cldots%24> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Anormalized-legendre-polynomials> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Anormalized-legendre-polynomials/given%20by%20%24%5Cvarphi_%7Bn%7D%20%3D%20y_%7Bn%7D%2F%5C%7Cy_%7Bn%7D%5C%7C%24%20are%20called%20the%20normalized%20Legendre%20polynomials.> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Anormalized-legendre-polynomials> <http://example.org/ch15/name_en> "Normalized Legendre polynomials"@en .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Anormalized-legendre-polynomials> <http://example.org/ch15/name_zh> "规范化 Legendre 多项式"@zh .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Anormalized-legendre-polynomials> <http://example.org/ch15/node_type> "concept" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Anormalized-legendre-polynomials> <http://example.org/ch15/section> "15.13" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Anormalized-legendre-polynomials> <http://example.org/ch15/statement> "The orthonormal sequence phi_0, phi_1, phi_2, ... given by phi_n = y_n/||y_n||, obtained by dividing each orthogonalized polynomial by its norm; for instance phi_0(t) = 1/sqrt 2 and phi_1(t) = sqrt(3/2) t." .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Anormalizing-an-element> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Anormalizing-an-element/Dividing%20each%20of%20%24y_%7B1%7D%24%20and%20%24y_%7B2%7D%24%20by%20its%20norm%20we%20get%20an%20orthonormal%20basis%20consisting%20of%20the%20two%20vectors> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Anormalizing-an-element> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Anormalizing-an-element/We%20can%20also%20convert%20this%20to%20an%20orthonormal%20basis%20by%20normalizing%20each%20element%20%24y_%7Bi%7D%24%20%2C%20that%20is%2C%20by%20dividing%20it%20by%20its%20norm.> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Anormalizing-an-element> <http://example.org/ch15/name_en> "Normalizing an element"@en .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Anormalizing-an-element> <http://example.org/ch15/name_zh> "元素的规范化(单位化)"@zh .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Anormalizing-an-element> <http://example.org/ch15/node_type> "method" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Anormalizing-an-element> <http://example.org/ch15/section> "15.13" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Anormalizing-an-element> <http://example.org/ch15/statement> "Dividing a nonzero element y_i of a Euclidean space by its norm ||y_i||, which converts an orthogonal set or orthogonal basis into an orthonormal one. It is the second half of the standard construction behind Theorem 15.14 and produces the normalized Legendre polynomials in Example 2." .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-basis> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Aorthogonal-basis/Every%20finite-dimensional%20linear%20space%20has%20a%20finite%20basis.%20If%20the%20space%20is%20Euclidean%2C%20we%20can%20always%20construct%20an%20orthogonal%20basis.> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-basis> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Aorthogonal-basis/The%20set%20%24%5C%7By_%7B1%7D%2C%20y_%7B2%7D%5C%7D%24%20is%20an%20orthogonal%20basis%20for%20this%20subspace.> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-basis> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Aorthogonal-basis/if%20%24x_%7B1%7D%2C%20%5Cldots%2C%20x_%7Bk%7D%24%20is%20a%20basis%20for%20a%20finite-dimensional%20Euclidean%20space%2C%20then%20%24y_%7B1%7D%2C%20%5Cldots%2C%20y_%7Bk%7D%24%20is%20an%20orthogonal%20basis%20for%20the%20same%20space.> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-basis> <http://example.org/ch15/name_en> "Orthogonal basis"@en .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-basis> <http://example.org/ch15/name_zh> "正交基"@zh .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-basis> <http://example.org/ch15/node_type> "concept" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-basis> <http://example.org/ch15/section> "15.13" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-basis> <http://example.org/ch15/statement> "A basis of a Euclidean space whose elements form an orthogonal set of nonzero elements. Applying the Gram-Schmidt process to a basis x_1, ..., x_k of a finite-dimensional Euclidean space produces an orthogonal basis y_1, ..., y_k of that same space, so every finite-dimensional Euclidean space has one." .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-sequence-unique-up-to-scalars> <http://example.org/ch15/alias> "THEOREM 15.13 (c)" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-sequence-unique-up-to-scalars> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Aorthogonal-sequence-unique-up-to-scalars/%28c%29%20The%20sequence%20%24y_1%2C%20y_2%2C%20%5Cldots%24%20%2C%20is%20unique%2C%20except%20for%20scalar%20factors.> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-sequence-unique-up-to-scalars> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Aorthogonal-sequence-unique-up-to-scalars/if%20%24y_1%27%2C%20y_2%27%2C%20%5Cldots%24%20%2C%20is%20another%20sequence%20of%20elements%20in%20%24V%24%20satisfying%20properties%20%28a%29%20and%20%28b%29%2C%20then%20for%20each%20%24k%24%20there%20is%20a%20scalar%20%24c_k%24%20such%20that%20%24y_k%27%20%3D%20c_k%20y_k%24%20.> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-sequence-unique-up-to-scalars> <http://example.org/ch15/name_en> "Uniqueness of the orthogonalized sequence up to scalar factors"@en .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-sequence-unique-up-to-scalars> <http://example.org/ch15/name_zh> "正交化序列在相差标量因子下的唯一性"@zh .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-sequence-unique-up-to-scalars> <http://example.org/ch15/node_type> "theorem" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-sequence-unique-up-to-scalars> <http://example.org/ch15/section> "15.13" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthogonal-sequence-unique-up-to-scalars> <http://example.org/ch15/statement> "Part (c) of the orthogonalization theorem: if y_1', y_2', ... is another sequence in V satisfying properties (a) and (b), then for each k there is a scalar c_k with y_k' = c_k y_k. The proof writes y_{r+1}' = z_r + c_{r+1} y_{r+1} with z_r in L(y_1, ..., y_r) and shows z_r is orthogonal to itself, hence z_r = O." .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthonormal-basis> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Aorthonormal-basis/Dividing%20each%20of%20%24y_%7B1%7D%24%20and%20%24y_%7B2%7D%24%20by%20its%20norm%20we%20get%20an%20orthonormal%20basis%20consisting%20of%20the%20two%20vectors> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthonormal-basis> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Aorthonormal-basis/In%20%24V_%7B4%7D%24%20%2C%20find%20an%20orthonormal%20basis%20for%20the%20subspace%20spanned%20by%20the%20three%20vectors> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthonormal-basis> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Aorthonormal-basis/We%20can%20also%20convert%20this%20to%20an%20orthonormal%20basis%20by%20normalizing%20each%20element%20%24y_%7Bi%7D%24%20%2C%20that%20is%2C%20by%20dividing%20it%20by%20its%20norm.> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthonormal-basis> <http://example.org/ch15/name_en> "Orthonormal basis"@en .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthonormal-basis> <http://example.org/ch15/name_zh> "正交规范基"@zh .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthonormal-basis> <http://example.org/ch15/node_type> "concept" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthonormal-basis> <http://example.org/ch15/section> "15.13" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aorthonormal-basis> <http://example.org/ch15/statement> "An orthogonal basis in which every element has norm 1, obtained from an orthogonal basis by dividing each element by its norm. Example 1 exhibits the orthonormal basis {(1/2)(1,-1,1,-1), (1/sqrt 6)(2,1,0,1)} for a two-dimensional subspace of V_4." .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aprojection-along-an-element> <http://example.org/ch15/alias> "projection of x along y" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aprojection-along-an-element> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Aprojection-along-an-element/If%20%24x%24%20and%20%24y%24%20are%20elements%20in%20a%20Euclidean%20space%2C%20with%20%24y%20%5Cneq%20O%24%20%2C%20the%20element> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aprojection-along-an-element> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Aprojection-along-an-element/In%20the%20Gram-Schmidt%20process%20%2815.15%29%2C%20we%20construct%20the%20element%20%24y_%7Br%2B1%7D%24%20by%20subtracting%20from%20%24x_%7Br%2B1%7D%24%20the%20projection%20of%20%24x_%7Br%2B1%7D%24%20along%20each%20of%20the%20earlier%20elements%20%24y_%7B1%7D%2C%5Cldots%2Cy_%7Br%7D%24%20.> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aprojection-along-an-element> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Aprojection-along-an-element/is%20called%20the%20projection%20of%20x%20along%20y.> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aprojection-along-an-element> <http://example.org/ch15/name_en> "Projection of x along y"@en .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aprojection-along-an-element> <http://example.org/ch15/name_zh> "x 沿 y 方向的投影"@zh .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aprojection-along-an-element> <http://example.org/ch15/node_type> "concept" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aprojection-along-an-element> <http://example.org/ch15/section> "15.13" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aprojection-along-an-element> <http://example.org/ch15/section> "15.14" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Aprojection-along-an-element> <http://example.org/ch15/statement> "For elements x and y of a Euclidean space with y not the zero element, the element ((x, y)/(y, y)) y is called the projection of x along y. The Gram-Schmidt step forms y_{r+1} by subtracting from x_{r+1} its projection along each earlier y_i. In 15.14 the same notion is applied to an orthonormal basis element, where (y, y) = 1 reduces the coefficient to (x, e_i)." .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Atheorem-15-13-orthogonalization-theorem> <http://example.org/ch15/alias> "Orthogonalization theorem" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Atheorem-15-13-orthogonalization-theorem> <http://example.org/ch15/alias> "THEOREM 15.13" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Atheorem-15-13-orthogonalization-theorem> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Atheorem-15-13-orthogonalization-theorem/%28a%29%20The%20element%20%24y_%7Bk%7D%24%20is%20orthogonal%20to%20every%20element%20in%20the%20subspace%20%24L%28y_%7B1%7D%2C%5Cldots%20%2Cy_%7Bk%20-%201%7D%29%24%20.> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Atheorem-15-13-orthogonalization-theorem> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Atheorem-15-13-orthogonalization-theorem/%28b%29%20The%20subspace%20spanned%20by%20%24y_%7B1%7D%2C%20%5Cldots%2C%20y_%7Bk%7D%24%20is%20the%20same%20as%20that%20spanned%20by%20%24x_%7B1%7D%2C%20%5Cldots%2C%20x_%7Bk%7D%24%20%3A> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Atheorem-15-13-orthogonalization-theorem> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Atheorem-15-13-orthogonalization-theorem/THEOREM%2015.13.%20ORTHOGONALIZATION%20THEOREM.%20Let%20%24x_1%2C%20x_2%2C%20%5Cldots%2C%24%20be%20a%20finite%20or%20infinite%20sequence%20of%20elements%20in%20a%20Euclidean%20space%20%24V%24%20%2C> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Atheorem-15-13-orthogonalization-theorem> <http://example.org/ch15/name_en> "Orthogonalization theorem (Theorem 15.13)"@en .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Atheorem-15-13-orthogonalization-theorem> <http://example.org/ch15/name_zh> "正交化定理(定理 15.13)"@zh .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Atheorem-15-13-orthogonalization-theorem> <http://example.org/ch15/node_type> "theorem" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Atheorem-15-13-orthogonalization-theorem> <http://example.org/ch15/section> "15.13" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Atheorem-15-13-orthogonalization-theorem> <http://example.org/ch15/statement> "For any finite or infinite sequence x_1, x_2, ... in a Euclidean space V there is a corresponding sequence y_1, y_2, ... in V such that for every k: (a) y_k is orthogonal to every element of L(y_1, ..., y_{k-1}); (b) L(y_1, ..., y_k) = L(x_1, ..., x_k); and (c) the sequence is unique except for scalar factors. Its proof is constructive and by induction, taking y_1 = x_1 and subtracting suitable multiples of the earlier y_i from x_{r+1}." .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Atheorem-15-14-orthonormal-basis-exists> <http://example.org/ch15/alias> "THEOREM 15.14" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Atheorem-15-14-orthonormal-basis-exists> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Atheorem-15-14-orthonormal-basis-exists/THEOREM%2015.14.%20Every%20finite-dimensional%20Euclidean%20space%20has%20an%20orthonormal%20basis.> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Atheorem-15-14-orthonormal-basis-exists> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Atheorem-15-14-orthonormal-basis-exists/Therefore%2C%20as%20a%20corollary%20of%20Theorem%2015.13%20we%20have%20the%20following.> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Atheorem-15-14-orthonormal-basis-exists> <http://example.org/ch15/name_en> "Every finite-dimensional Euclidean space has an orthonormal basis (Theorem 15.14)"@en .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Atheorem-15-14-orthonormal-basis-exists> <http://example.org/ch15/name_zh> "每个有限维欧几里得空间都有正交规范基(定理 15.14)"@zh .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Atheorem-15-14-orthonormal-basis-exists> <http://example.org/ch15/node_type> "theorem" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Atheorem-15-14-orthonormal-basis-exists> <http://example.org/ch15/section> "15.13" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Atheorem-15-14-orthonormal-basis-exists> <http://example.org/ch15/statement> "Theorem 15.14 states that every finite-dimensional Euclidean space has an orthonormal basis. It is obtained as a corollary of Theorem 15.13: orthogonalize a finite basis by the Gram-Schmidt process, then normalize each resulting element." .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Avanishing-orthogonalized-element-detects-dependence> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Avanishing-orthogonalized-element-detects-dependence/Since%20%24y_%7B3%7D%3DO%24%20%2C%20the%20three%20vectors%20%24x_%7B1%7D%2C%20x_%7B2%7D%2C%20x_%7B3%7D%24%20must%20be%20dependent.> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Avanishing-orthogonalized-element-detects-dependence> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Avanishing-orthogonalized-element-detects-dependence/Then%20%2815.13%29%20shows%20that%20%24x_%7Br%2B1%7D%24%20is%20a%20linear%20combination%20of%20%24y_%7B1%7D%2C%5Cldots%2Cy_%7Br%7D%24%20%2C%20and%20hence%20of%20%24x_%7B1%7D%2C%5Cldots%2Cx_%7Br%7D%24%20%2C%20so%20the%20elements%20%24x_%7B1%7D%2C%5Cldots%2Cx_%7Br%2B1%7D%24%20are%20dependent.> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Avanishing-orthogonalized-element-detects-dependence> <http://example.org/ch15/hasAnchor> <http://example.org/ch15/anchor/apostol%3Avanishing-orthogonalized-element-detects-dependence/if%20the%20first%20k%20elements%20%24x_%7B1%7D%2C%5Cldots%2Cx_%7Bk%7D%24%20are%20independent%2C%20then%20the%20corresponding%20elements%20%24y_%7B1%7D%2C%5Cldots%2Cy_%7Bk%7D%24%20are%20nonzero> .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Avanishing-orthogonalized-element-detects-dependence> <http://example.org/ch15/name_en> "A vanishing orthogonalized element detects dependence"@en .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Avanishing-orthogonalized-element-detects-dependence> <http://example.org/ch15/name_zh> "正交化结果为零即检出线性相依"@zh .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Avanishing-orthogonalized-element-detects-dependence> <http://example.org/ch15/node_type> "theorem" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Avanishing-orthogonalized-element-detects-dependence> <http://example.org/ch15/section> "15.13" .
<http://example.org/ch15/node/apostol%3Avanishing-orthogonalized-element-detects-dependence> <http://example.org/ch15/statement> "If the Gram-Schmidt construction yields y_{r+1} = O for some r, then x_{r+1} is a linear combination of y_1, ..., y_r and hence of x_1, ..., x_r, so x_1, ..., x_{r+1} are dependent. Equivalently, if the first k elements x_1, ..., x_k are independent then the corresponding y_1, ..., y_k are all nonzero, so the process doubles as an independence test." .
